[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Laptop0’s host routing configuration (default gateway).
Mechanism: Laptop0 can communicate within VLAN 20 (same subnet) via ARP and L2 switching, but any off-subnet traffic uses the default route. The default gateway on Laptop0 is set to 172.16.20.99, which is not present on the VLAN. ARP for 172.16.20.99 remains incomplete, so packets to off-subnet destinations never leave the host, resulting in “Destination Host Unreachable” sourced by 172.16.20.10. In-VLAN pings work because no gateway is needed.

Localization: On Laptop0, ip route shows “default via 172.16.20.99 dev eth0.” ARP shows 172.16.20.99 incomplete, and ping to 172.16.20.99 fails. By contrast, the real VLAN 20 gateway is router0’s subinterface eth0.20 at 172.16.20.1 (confirmed on router0). Laptop0 successfully pings 172.16.20.1, proving L2/VLAN plumbing is correct (switch1 eth7 is in br_vlan20 as expected), and that the upstream router SVI is reachable. VLAN 10 hosts (e.g., laptop2/laptop3) have correct defaults via 172.16.10.1 and are unaffected, matching the report.

Root cause: Laptop0’s default gateway is misconfigured to 172.16.20.99 instead of 172.16.20.1.

Corrective fix: On Laptop0, replace the default route with the correct gateway: “ip route del default; ip route add default via 172.16.20.1 dev eth0.” If using DHCP, correct the default-router option. Afterward, clear the ARP entry if needed (“ip neigh flush dev eth0”). This will restore off-VLAN connectivity. 
=== END ===